s = open('ms_results_xai.py', encoding='utf-8').read()
s = s.replace("hd = DG[dgs[0]]['hair_removal_deviation']; ist = DG[dgs[0]]['image_stats']",
              "hd = DG[dgs[0]]['hair_removal_deviation']; ist = json.load(open(RES / 'domain_gap_42.json'))['image_stats']")

i = s.index("    M.p(\"__Conclusion of the audit (Outcome C).__")
j = s.index("\n", s.index("is therefore withdrawn.", i)) + 1
new_size = '''    rho_gap = ac('perm_null', 'rho_obs') - ac('perm_null', 'rho_null_mean')
    M.p(f"__Conclusion of the audit (Outcome C, with a caveat).__ In {cnt(ac('perm_null', 'p_greater'), lambda v: v >= 0.05)}/{K} seeds the observed LSAS–area association was indistinguishable from, or below, the geometric null; in the remaining seed(s) it exceeded the null by a statistically detectable but negligible margin (observed − null mean of Spearman ρ across seeds: {rng_s(rho_gap, 3)}). Geometry therefore accounts for essentially all of the raw association in every seed. The large lesion-size effect reported in the original submission is, to a very large extent, a mathematical property of the LSAS statistic and the coarse CAM resolution, not evidence that the model explains small lesions worse. After removing the geometric expectation, a small residual enrichment above the model's spatial prior persists (median excess {rng_s(ac('enrichment_vs_null', 'all', 'median_e_shuf'), 3)} across seeds), and its dependence on lesion size is negative in every seed (ρ = {rng_s(ac('size_measures', 'e_shuf', 'spearman_area', 'rho'), 2)}), not the increase that the raw statistic suggests. The original statement that lesion size produced 'the strongest explanation effect' is therefore withdrawn.")
'''
s = s[:i] + new_size + s[j:]

i = s.index("    M.p(f\"__Results.__ In the original design")
j = s.index("    M.table(['Proxy version, subset'")
new_skin = '''    sk = lambda ver, sub, *p: np.array([g(XA[q]['skin'][f'{ver}_{sub}'], *p) for q in sorted(XA)], float)
    kwc_p = sk('v1', 'correct_only', 'kw_lsas', 'p'); kwe_p = sk('v1', 'all', 'kw_e_shuf', 'p'); rho_e = sk('v1', 'all', 'spearman_ita_eshuf', 'rho'); rho_e_p = sk('v1', 'all', 'spearman_ita_eshuf', 'p')
    adj_c = sk('v1', 'all', 'ols_eshuf_adj', 'coef_ita'); adj_p = sk('v1', 'all', 'ols_eshuf_adj', 'p')
    lowest = [int(XA[q]['skin']['v1_all']['med']['V-VI'] < min(XA[q]['skin']['v1_all']['med']['I-II'], XA[q]['skin']['v1_all']['med']['III-IV'])) for q in sorted(XA)]
    v1c = R['skin']['v1_correct_only']; v1a = R['skin']['v1_all']; mde = R['skin']['mde_correct_only']
    M.p(f"__Results.__ In the original design (correct predictions, v1 proxy) the Kruskal–Wallis test on raw LSAS across the three strata was significant at α = 0.05 in {cnt(kwc_p, lambda v: v < 0.05)}/{K} seeds (p range {kwc_p.min():.1e}–{kwc_p.max():.2f}; reference seed {REF}: {fp(v1c['kw_lsas']['p'])}). The darkest proxy stratum had the lowest median raw LSAS in {sum(lowest)}/{K} seeds (reference seed: {v1a['med']['V-VI']:.3f} vs {v1a['med']['I-II']:.3f} for I–II), but this direction is that of the area geometry, since darker-proxy images tend to have smaller lesions (ρ(ITA, area) = {v1a['spearman_ita_area']['rho']:+.3f} in the reference seed). After subtracting the geometric expectation, excess LSAS differed significantly between strata in {cnt(kwe_p, lambda v: v < 0.05)}/{K} seeds (all-image analysis; p range {kwe_p.min():.1e}–{kwe_p.max():.2f}); the association of continuous ITA with excess LSAS ranged from ρ = {rho_e.min():+.2f} to {rho_e.max():+.2f} across seeds and changed sign between seeds (significant at α = 0.05 in {cnt(rho_e_p, lambda v: v < 0.05)}/{K} seeds, with both signs), and the adjusted slope (excess LSAS on ITA, log area and class) ranged from {adj_c.min():+.5f} to {adj_c.max():+.5f} per degree (p < 0.05 in {cnt(adj_p, lambda v: v < 0.05)}/{K} seeds; reference seed {v1a['ols_eshuf_adj']['coef_ita']:+.5f}, 95% CI {v1a['ols_eshuf_adj']['ci'][0]:+.5f} to {v1a['ols_eshuf_adj']['ci'][1]:+.5f}). Where a seed produced a significant difference, it was not monotonic in tone (in seed 43 the medium stratum, not the darkest, was the lowest in excess LSAS) and it did not replicate in the other seeds. With the vignette-robust v2 proxy the raw contrasts were significant in {cnt(sk('v2', 'all', 'kw_lsas', 'p'), lambda v: v < 0.05)}/{K} seeds. The darkest stratum is too small for anything but a large effect: with {mde['n_I_II']} (I–II) versus {mde['n_V_VI']} (V–VI) correct predictions the minimum detectable standardized difference at 80% power is d = {mde['cohen_d_80pct']:.2f}, about {mde['lsas_units']:.3f} LSAS units (SD {mde['lsas_sd']:.3f}); the rank-biserial effect of I–II versus V–VI on raw LSAS was {v1c['pairwise_lsas'][1]['rbc']:+.2f} in the reference seed with a Holm-adjusted p of {v1c['pairwise_lsas'][1]['p_holm']:.2f}. Accuracy by proxy stratum is shown in Table 12; the intervals are wide and the ordering of strata is not what a monotonic tone effect would predict.")
    M.p("__Interpretation.__ The evidence is best described as inconclusive: it is neither absence of an effect nor evidence of one. The raw LSAS difference in the darkest stratum is directionally consistent but is explained by lesion geometry; area-adjusted associations are small, change sign between independently trained models, and reach nominal significance in isolated seeds. That seed-to-seed instability is itself informative: findings from a single training run in a stratum of about 30 images should not be trusted in either direction. The analyses do not show that explanations are fair across skin tones: the proxy is not a clinical skin type, the darkest stratum is tiny and differs between two proxy definitions, power is limited to large effects, and there are no darker-skin dermoscopic images from independent sources. 'No consistent association detected', 'insufficient evidence' and 'fairness validated' are different statements, and only the first is supported. The earlier held-out tone-calibration analysis of LSAS thresholds was not repeated: because quantile mapping is designed to remove observed gaps, it cannot serve as evidence about fairness.")
'''
s = s[:i] + new_skin + s[j:]
open('ms_results_xai.py', 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(s)

b = open('build_manuscript.py', encoding='utf-8').read()
b = b.replace("check(all(XA[s]['perm_null']['p_greater'] > 0.05 for s in XA), 'raw LSAS-area correlation exceeds geometric null in some seed')",
              "check(sum(XA[s]['perm_null']['p_greater'] < 0.05 for s in XA) <= 1 and max(XA[s]['perm_null']['rho_obs'] - XA[s]['perm_null']['rho_null_mean'] for s in XA) < 0.02, 'raw LSAS-area correlation exceeds geometric null materially')")
b = b.replace("check(all(XA[s]['perm_null']['p_H'] > 0.05 for s in XA), 'raw KW exceeds null in some seed')\n", "")
b = b.replace("check(all(XA[s]['skin']['v1_correct_only']['kw_lsas']['p'] > 0.05 for s in XA), 'ITA KW significant in some seed (correct-only v1)')\n", "")
open('build_manuscript.py', 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(b)
print('patched')
